.TH WM-SHELL 1
.SH NAME
wm/shell \- the shell in a window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/shell
[
.B -ilxvn
] [
.B -w
.I width
] [
.B -h
.I height
] [
.B -f
.I font
] [
.B -c
.I command
] [
.I file
[
.IR arg ...
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wm/shell
runs
.IR sh (1)
in a window, with a
.B /dev/cons
and
.B /dev/consctl
of the window's own.
The window's text is the transcript:
what programs print goes in at the end of the output,
and what is typed after it is the input,
sent to a program reading
.B /dev/cons
a line at a time.
Text typed elsewhere in the window edits the transcript and is not sent.
It is
.B wm/sh
with the theme's colours and font, following a theme switch
(its text, background and selection as Xenith's body has them:
.BR edittext ,
.B editbg
and
.BR menuhilit ;
the text is not dimmed when the window has not the keyboard),
a history of the lines sent,
and the transcript kept where Veltro's shell tool reads it;
.B wm/wm
starts one for
.BR New .
.PP
The options
.BR -i ,
.BR -l ,
.BR -x ,
.BR -v ,
.BR -n ,
.B -c
.IR command ,
and
.I file
and its
.I args
are given to
.IR sh (1)
(which is always given
.BR -n :
it shares the window's name space).
.B -w
and
.B -h
give the window's size in pixels (640 by 480 by default),
.B -f
its font, under
.B /fonts
unless the name begins with
.B /
or
.BR ./ .
When the shell exits, as when a
.B -c
command is done, the window goes;
when the window is closed, the shell and what it runs go with it.
.SS Keyboard
In cooked mode, the default:
.TF Enter
.TP
.B Enter
Send the line.
.TP
.B ^D
Send what is typed without a newline; on its own, end of file.
.TP
.B ^H
Erase the character before the caret.
.TP
.B ^W
Erase the word before the caret.
.TP
.B ^U
Erase the line before the caret.
.TP
.B ^C
Discard what is typed and not yet sent, and interrupt what the shell is
running: every process the shell started is killed (the shell runs in a
process group of its own; a program that made its own, as a window
does, is left), and the shell ends that line (see
.IR sh (1))
and prompts again.
.TP
.BR ^P ", " ^N
Replace the line being typed with the previous, or the next, line sent.
.TP
.B ^L
Clear the transcript above the line being written.
.TP
.B Esc
Hold: nothing typed is sent until
.B Esc
again; the text is drawn in the theme's
.B edithold
colour meanwhile, and the title says
.BR (holding) .
.PD
.PP
The arrows,
.BR Home ,
.BR End ,
.BR "Page Up" ,
.BR "Page Down" ,
.BR ^A ,
.BR ^E ,
.BR ^B ,
.BR ^F
and
.B ^K
are the text widget's own.
After a program writes
.B rawon
to
.BR /dev/consctl ,
every key goes to it as typed and is not shown,
until it writes
.B rawoff
or closes the file.
.B holdon
and
.B holdoff
set and clear hold.
.SS Mouse
Button 1 places the caret and selects.
Button 2 shows the menu:
.TF noscroll
.TP
.B noscroll
Stop moving to the end of the output as it comes; while the end is
out of sight, programs writing to the window wait.
.B scroll
undoes it.
.TP
.B cut
Delete the selection, keeping it in the snarf buffer.
.TP
.B paste
Replace the selection with the snarf buffer.
.TP
.B snarf
Copy the selection to the snarf buffer.
.TP
.B send
Send the selection, or the snarf buffer, as typed, with a newline.
.PD
.PP
Button 3, released, plumbs the selection if it is under the pointer,
or the word under the pointer, from the window's directory (see
.IR plumber (8)).
With button 1 held, button 2 cuts and button 3 pastes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /chan/shctl
Programs in the window write commands here:
.B button
.I label text
adds a button above the text that sends
.I text
as typed;
.B action
.I label text
adds one whose
.I text
a read of
.B /chan/shctl
returns;
.B clear
removes the buttons;
.B cwd
.I dir
sets the directory shown in the title and plumbed from.
.TP
.B #sshell/fs
For Veltro's
.B shell
tool (mounted at
.B /mnt/shell
by
.IR tools9p (8)):
.B body
is the transcript up to the end of the output and
.B input
what is typed and not yet read, both read from the window as it is;
.B event
waits for the next change.  A second shell is
.BR #sshell/fs.1 .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/shell.b
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR sh (1),
.IR wm (1),
